{"t":1791405444,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"eb9f373a-ee57-4a61-9a27-9ab2e686283f","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-07T19:26:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 071926\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n326 PM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nForecast for remnants of Isaias over our area trending drier \nand a little slower Sunday evening. SCA has been issued for\ntonight for the waters south of Drum Point.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Quiescent weather expected through the end of the week.\n\n- 2) Remnants of Tropical Storm Isaias arrive late this \n     weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiescent weather expected through the end of\nthe week.\n\nMilder night on tap as southerly winds prevail ahead of a dry\ncold frontal passage Thu morning. Warmer tomorrow as gusty\ndownslope NW winds develop in the wake of the frontal passage. \n\nCooler weather to end the week as high pressure of Canadian\norigin builds in. High pressure center settles in by Saturday\nmorning with low temps in the 40s and 50s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Remnants of Tropical Storm Isaias arrive late this \nweekend.\n\nAs of today, the National Hurricane Center is now issuing advisories \non Tropical Storm Isaias down in the Bay of Campeche. This Tropical \ncyclone will need to be monitored closely in the coming days in \nregards to potential impacts to our region late this weekend. There \nremains a great deal of uncertainty, owing to a complex upper-level \npattern ahead of it. A cutoff low over the southern Plains and an \narea of high pressure over Florida will act as the primary steering \nmechanisms in the coming days. The big questions are going to be... \nwhere does it go, and how fast does does it get there? A track \ndirectly over our area is highly unlikely at this time. However, it \ndoes seem likely to track somewhere up into the MS River Valley and \nthen eventually up towards the Great Lakes as it transitions into a \nnon-tropical low. \n\nAt present, the most likely impacts to our region will be rain. \nHowever, there isn't a great concern for flooding at this time, as \nthe system should be progressive and much of the region could use a \ngood soaking rain to help with drought relief. That being said, this \nsystem is still several days out, so we will be monitoring any \nrainfall trends closely as the time draws closer. There is also a \nnon-zero, but very small, chance that some severe weather could \noccur as a result of the remnants of Isaias. However, again, this \nwill greatly depend on how the upper-level pattern evolves and \ninteracts with Isaias as it moves into our region. Main threat, if \nit materializes would be the non-zero threat of a quick tornado on \nSunday afternoon/evening. \n\nAll of this to say, we just simply don't know if Isaias will bring \nany significant impacts our way. However, as is the case with any \ntropical cyclone, it will be important to stay tuned to the forecast \nover the next few days. Monitor hurricanes.gov for details on \nIsaias. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /19Z WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\n\nMain wx change next 24 hrs is windshift Thu morning behind a dry\ncold frontal passage. Otherwise, P6SM SKC through 21Z Fri. Then,\nincreasing high clouds Fri night.\n\nVFR conditions are expected Saturday as high pressure remains \ndominant over the region. Winds will be out of the S to SE on \nSaturday at 5 to 10 knots. \n\nMore uncertainty going into Sunday, but sub-VFR conditions are \npossible if we do manage to get any rain associated with the area of \nlow pressure over the southwestern Gulf of America. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\n\nSoutherly winds are fcst to strengthen this evening across the\nwaters tonight. Have issued a Small Craft Advisory for tonight\nfor the waters south of Drum Point. Winds tomorrow could flirt\nwith SCA across the northern waters. Winds drop below SCA by Thu\nafternoon.\n\nWinds are expected to remain below SCA criteria on Saturday as high \npressure remains dominant over the region. However, by Saturday \nevening, we could see a brief burst of southerly channeling winds \nthat come close to criteria. Confidence is low at this time. \n\nWinds will be on the increase on Sunday ahead of the remnants of \nIsaias, with high-end SCA conditions possible. SCAs seem likely for \nmuch of the day and into Sunday night. \n\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 8 AM EDT Thursday for ANZ534-543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/CJL\nAVIATION...LFR/CJL\nMARINE...LFR/CJL\n"},"forecast":{"t":1791405374,"days":{"2026-10-07":{"hi":72,"lo":57,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-08":{"hi":78,"lo":54,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-09":{"hi":75,"lo":56,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-10":{"hi":75,"lo":60,"pop":94,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-11":{"hi":68,"lo":64,"pop":95,"short":"Rain Showers"},"2026-10-12":{"hi":74,"lo":60,"pop":25,"short":"Chance Rain Showers then Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-10-13":{"hi":73,"lo":50,"pop":16,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1791403200,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0],"spd":[9,9,9,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,8,9,10,11,12,13,13,12,12,9,7,5,5,5,6,6,5,5,5,5,5,5,4,4,5,6,7,7,7,7,7],"gst":[10,10,10,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,8,8,8,8,8,9,10,13,14,16,17,17,16,16,10,8,6,6,6,7,7,6,6,6,6,6,6,5,5,6,7,8,8,8,8,8],"